      title: "Kansas, Nebraska, and the Republican Party",
      body: `## Douglas's bill

In January 1854, Senator Stephen A. Douglas introduced a bill to organize the territory west of Missouri and Iowa, which lay north of 36°30' and was therefore closed to slavery under the Missouri Compromise. Douglas wanted the territory organized so that a transcontinental railroad could be built along a central route, from Chicago, rather than a southern one; he had investments in Chicago real estate and presidential ambitions. To win Southern votes, he agreed to provisions that would organize two territories, **Kansas** and **Nebraska**, under the principle of **popular sovereignty**, and to repeal explicitly the Missouri Compromise's prohibition of slavery north of 36°30'.

Douglas believed that slavery would never take root in the northern plains, and that popular sovereignty was a democratic principle that would remove the question from Congress. He predicted that the bill would "raise a hell of a storm." It did. Antislavery congressmen issued an "**Appeal of the Independent Democrats**," denouncing the bill as "a gross violation of a sacred pledge" and "part and parcel of an atrocious plot" to turn free territory into "a dreary region of despotism, inhabited by masters and slaves." After months of bitter debate, the **Kansas-Nebraska Act** passed in May 1854, with the support of President Franklin Pierce and nearly every Southern member of Congress, and only about half of Northern Democrats.

## The collapse of the party system

The act shattered the second party system. The Whig Party, already weakened by sectional divisions, disintegrated: its Southern members had voted for the bill, its Northern members against it. Northern Democrats who had supported it were slaughtered in the congressional elections of 1854.

For a moment it seemed that the beneficiary would be the **American Party**, known as the Know-Nothings because members of its secret lodges answered questions with "I know nothing." The Know-Nothings, whom you met in AMH 201, appealed to Protestant voters alarmed by the surge of Catholic immigration, and in 1854–55 they won control of several state governments, including Massachusetts. But the Know-Nothings, too, divided over slavery.

## The Republicans

The lasting beneficiary was a new party. In 1854, meetings across the North, in places like Ripon, Wisconsin, and Jackson, Michigan, organized a new party opposed to the extension of slavery, which took the name **Republican**, recalling Jefferson. It drew together antislavery Whigs, Free Soilers, anti-Nebraska Democrats, and many Know-Nothings. It was the first major party in American history whose support was confined to one section.

The Republicans were not abolitionists. They accepted that the Constitution protected slavery in the states where it existed. But they insisted that Congress must prohibit its expansion into the territories, which they believed would put slavery, in Lincoln's phrase, "in the course of ultimate extinction." As the historian **Eric Foner** showed in *Free Soil, Free Labor, Free Men* (1970), the Republicans' ideology centered on **free labor**: the belief that the North's society of independent farmers, artisans, and wage earners who could rise to independence was morally and economically superior to the South's slave society, which degraded labor, kept poor whites in ignorance, and stifled progress. They feared that a "Slave Power"—a conspiracy of slaveholders who controlled the federal government—was determined to spread slavery across the West and even into the North. Many Republicans also wanted the territories reserved for white settlers; antislavery and racism were often combined.

## Bleeding Kansas

Popular sovereignty turned Kansas into a battleground. Antislavery Northerners, aided by the New England Emigrant Aid Company, sent settlers to Kansas to make it free. Proslavery Missourians, called "**border ruffians**" by their enemies, crossed into Kansas to vote. In the territorial election of March 1855, thousands of Missourians voted illegally, and a proslavery legislature was elected, which met at Lecompton and passed harsh laws, including the death penalty for aiding a fugitive slave. Free-state settlers refused to recognize this "bogus legislature," and formed their own government at Topeka. Kansas had two governments, each claiming legitimacy.

Violence followed. On May 21, 1856, a proslavery posse sacked the free-state town of **Lawrence**, destroying newspaper presses and burning the hotel and the home of the free-state governor. Three days later, the abolitionist **John Brown**, with four of his sons and a few others, took five proslavery settlers from their cabins along Pottawatomie Creek and hacked them to death with broadswords. Guerrilla warfare spread. By the end of 1856, dozens had died, and "**Bleeding Kansas**" had become a national symbol.

## Bleeding Sumner

The violence reached the Senate. On May 19–20, 1856, Senator **Charles Sumner** of Massachusetts delivered a speech, "The Crime against Kansas," denouncing the proslavery forces in lurid language and insulting the elderly Senator Andrew Butler of South Carolina, whom he accused of taking "the harlot, Slavery," as his mistress. Two days later, Butler's kinsman, Congressman **Preston Brooks**, walked into the Senate chamber and beat Sumner over the head with a cane until he collapsed, bloody and unconscious. Sumner did not return to the Senate for over three years. Brooks resigned, was reelected overwhelmingly, and received dozens of new canes from admirers across the South. In the North, "Bleeding Kansas" and "Bleeding Sumner" seemed proof that the Slave Power would stop at nothing.

![A cartoon of Preston Brooks beating Charles Sumner with a cane on the Senate floor while other senators watch and laugh.](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Southern_chivalry_-_argument_versus_club%27s.jpg "John L. Magee, *Southern Chivalry: Argument versus Club's* (1856). A Northern view of the caning of Sumner: the senator holds a pen; his attacker, a club.")

## The election of 1856

In 1856 the Republicans nominated the explorer **John C. Frémont**, under the slogan "Free Soil, Free Speech, Free Men, and Frémont." The Democrats nominated **James Buchanan** of Pennsylvania, who had been abroad as minister to Britain during the Kansas-Nebraska debate and thus untainted by it. The Know-Nothings nominated former president Fillmore. Buchanan won, carrying the South and five Northern states. But Frémont carried eleven Northern states, a stunning achievement for a party only two years old. Southerners warned that a Republican victory would mean disunion.

> **Pause and consider.** Douglas believed popular sovereignty was democratic and would remove slavery from national politics. Why did it have the opposite effect?`,
